amy_cs: hi team — just escalated a support ticket (INC-4821). Customer using /v1/rerank keeps getting either 400/422 or very low uniform scores (all 0.01). They sent a payload that worked previously but now fails after they refactored their indexing. sample from customer:

amy_cs: ```json
{
  "query": "how to reset password",
  "documents": [
    {
      "id": "doc_123",
      "query": "reset password steps",
      "passages": [
        {"text": "Step 1: go to settings..."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doc_124",
      "passages": [ {"text": "Contact support..."} ]
    }
  ]
}
```

amy_cs: they said error is "invalid payload: ambiguous query key" for some requests and other times it returns scores but they are tiny and identical across candidates.

sam_engineering: curious — looks like they stuck a per-document "query" field which collides with the top-level query name. Our rerank schema expects either a top-level "query" and a list of "candidates" (or "passages" depending on mode). If the client nests a field named "query" inside docs the server can pick the wrong field during flattening.

lee_ml: add to that: depending on which internal adapter path runs (doc->passage vs candidate list), we normalize fields differently. We used to ignore inner "query" but recent strict validation caught it and returned 422. That would also explain the "uniform scores" when the scoring pipeline receives empty candidate text (it falls back to default vector zeros).

jordan_product: do we have a simple repro we can give the customer? they want a minimal fix or a short-term workaround while they update their exporter.

sam_engineering: yes. repro (what to send them):

sam_engineering: ```bash
# BAD (their current payload)
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.com/v1/rerank -d '{
  "query":"how to reset password",
  "documents":[{
     "id":"doc_1",
     "query":"internal query text",
     "passages":[{"text":"..."}]
  }]
}'

# GOOD (explicit candidates)
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.com/v1/rerank -d '{
  "query":"how to reset password",
  "candidates":[
    {"id":"doc_1","text":"Step 1: go to settings and click reset"},
    {"id":"doc_2","text":"Contact support to reset your password"}
  ]
}'
```

sam_engineering: also note limits — pass candidates as "candidates" when you want per-item text. If you need to send documents with multiple passages, use the "passages" array but do NOT reuse field names like "query" at nested levels.

lee_ml: for the customer: short-term workaround is to rename their nested field (e.g., "doc_query") before calling the API. We'll also add a clearer validation error message to point out "nested query collision".

jordan_product: can we fast-track a small change to validation to return a clearer 422 string like: "Nested field \"query\" found in documents. Use \"candidates\" or rename inner fields. See docs.redwood.com/rerank-schema"?

sam_engineering: yes we can. I'll open an eng ticket (ENG-9982) to tweak validation and add a note to the API docs.

ops_bot: Incident linked: INC-4821 -> ENG-9982, assigned to @sam_engineering.

amy_cs: I messaged the customer with repro and the temporary rename workaround. They'll test in ~30m and report back. I'll update the ticket. :thumbsup:

lee_ml: FYI also flagged that we should check the legacy doc->candidate conversion path for other name collisions (e.g., "id", "score", "metadata"). Might be a source of other subtle regressions.

amy_cs: thanks everyone. keeping this thread open until customer confirms.
